<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="indole-3-acetate inactivation IX" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The plant hormone |FRAME: INDOLE_ACETATE_AUXIN| (IAA) can be inactivated via multiple mechanisms A common mechanism involves in non-decarboxylative oxidation. |FRAME: TAX-4577| readily oxidizes free IAA (|FRAME: PWY-1961|) whereas |FRAME: TAX-47664| (hybrid aspen) oxidizes |FRAME: INDOLE-3-ACETYL-ASP| (IAA-asp) (|FRAME: PWY-1962|). |FRAME: TAX-3702| oxidizes both free IAA and IAA-asp (|FRAME: PWY-2021|). Other inactivation mechanisms involve direct glycosylation (|FRAME: PWY-1741|), hydroxylation followed by &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosylation (|FRAME: PWY-1981|, |FRAME: PWY-5811|), &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-glucosylation (|FRAME: PWY-5797|), and conjugation with different amino acids (|FRAME: PWY-6219|), which could be followed by further hydroxylation (|FRAME: PWY-5788|). &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; There are two types of IAA conjugates: ester and amide conjugates. The ester conjugates are formed with sugars or polyols through an oxygen bridge, whereas the amide conjugates are formed with amino acids or polypeptides through an amide bond. The content of conjugates varies among different plants. The majority of IAA conjugates in |FRAME: TAX-4577| endosperm are ester conjugates, whereas |FRAME: TAX-3702| leaves contain mainly amide conjugates. IAA conjugates play important roles in IAA homeostasis. They could serve as either intermediates for further degradation, or storage forms for the free IAA. Both ester and amide conjugates have been reported to be converted back to free IAA by hydrolysis. The biosynthetic pathway of IAA conjugates was best studied in maize endosperm. The first reaction of the pathway, which is energetically unfavorable, is coupled with the next energetically favorable reaction to drive the pathway. Thus maize kernels contain small amounts conjugates of IAA and glucose. The IAA and myo-inositol conjugate can be further glycosylated as shown here.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="xu" />
    <Author name="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE" />
    <Author name="zhang" />
  </Authors>
